---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import InstallLine from '../components/InstallLine.astro';
import { SURFACES, KEY_VARIABLE, HOWTOS } from '../data/catalog.mjs';
import { cell, HOWTO_RUNS } from '../data/examples.mjs';

const shell = SURFACES.find((s) => s.slug === 'shell');
const decide = cell('decide', 'shell').runs;
const run = (name) => decide.find((r) => r.name === name);

const plain = run('decide');
const answer = run('decide-answer');
const lines = run('decide-lines');
const branch = run('decide-case');

const saved = cell('question-file', 'shell').runs;
const savedYes = saved.find((r) => r.name === 'question-yes');
const savedUnsure = saved.find((r) => r.name === 'question-unsure');

const kept = cell('filter', 'shell').runs.find((r) => r.name === 'filter');
const sorted = cell('rank', 'shell').runs.find((r) => r.name === 'rank');
const triage = HOWTO_RUNS['triage-a-support-inbox'];

// The question file and the form come from the recorded runs, so the page
// shows the bytes the runs read.
const refundFile = savedYes.also.find((f) => f.name === 'refund.json').text;
const triageForm = triage.forms[0];
---
<Base title="Tutorial" description="From the install line to a pipeline that sorts a stream of messages.">
  <h1>Tutorial</h1>
  <p class="lede">This tutorial takes you from installing ThinkThen to a pipeline that sorts a stream of messages. You ask one question, read the number behind the answer, set your own bar, and save the question to a file.</p>

  <p>Every run below was recorded from the real command.</p>

  <h2>1. Install it</h2>
  <p>Both channels come with 0.1.</p>
  <InstallLine line={shell.install[0][0]} status="planned" label="comes with 0.1" note={shell.install[0][1]} caption="Homebrew tap" />
  <InstallLine line={shell.install[1][0]} status="planned" label="comes with 0.1" note={shell.install[1][1]} caption="Download script" />
  <p>Set <code>{KEY_VARIABLE}</code> in the environment. No flag and no file carries a key. Ask for a key before you start. While you wait, <code>--dry-run</code> shows what would be sent. It needs no key. The <a href="/install/">install page</a> carries every other surface.</p>

  <h2>2. Ask one question</h2>
  <p>Pipe the message in and put the question in the arguments. <code>decide</code> prints <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, or <code>null</code>, and it sets the exit code to match.</p>
  <Terminal run={plain} caption="Recorded from the real command. decide prints true and exits 0." />
  <p>The exit code is the answer. A script branches on it the way it branches on <code>test</code>.</p>

  <h2>3. Look at the number</h2>
  <p><code>true</code> hides a number. <code>--details</code> prints the whole result, and the model's probability sits under <code>.answer</code>.</p>
  <Terminal run={answer} caption="Recorded from the real command. The model puts yes at 0.99." />
  <p>That number is the model's own. The tool passes it through and computes nothing.</p>

  <h2>4. Set a band</h2>
  <p><code>--threshold</code> is your bar on the probability. One number is a cut, and the default is 0.5. Two numbers make a band: under the low bar is no, over the high bar is yes, and anything between is not sure.</p>
  <p>Three messages go in, one per line. A plain refund request is yes. A thank-you is no. "I want to send this back." could mean an exchange or money back, so it lands in the band.</p>
  <Terminal run={lines} caption={`Recorded from the real command. ${lines.see}`} />
  <p>The threshold is an input. The model never chooses it, and it never changes the probability. It changes what you call a yes.</p>

  <h2>5. Branch on the answer</h2>
  <p>Not sure prints <code>null</code> and exits 3. A script reads the exit code and hands a person the calls it should not make alone.</p>
  <Terminal run={branch} caption={`Recorded from the real command. ${branch.see}`} />

  <h2>6. Save the question to a file</h2>
  <p>A question worth tuning is worth keeping. A question file holds one question: the words, what a yes and a no mean, and the threshold.</p>
  <Code code={refundFile} status="run" caption="refund.json, the file the two runs below read." copy={true} />
  <p>Write <code>@refund.json</code> in place of the question words. A clear message passes.</p>
  <Terminal run={savedYes} caption="Recorded from the real command. The clear message answers yes." />
  <p>The send-back message could mean an exchange or money back. It lands inside the band and goes to a person.</p>
  <Terminal run={savedUnsure} caption="Recorded from the real command. The send-back message prints null and exits 3." />
  <p>Tune the file once. Your git hook, your test, and your pipeline then ask the same question.</p>

  <h2>7. Judge a stream</h2>
  <p>One of <code>--lines</code>, <code>--jsonl</code>, <code>--csv</code>, or <code>--tsv</code> says how the records are framed. <code>filter</code> keeps the records where the answer is yes, byte for byte and in order.</p>
  <Terminal run={kept} caption={`Recorded from the real command. ${kept.see}`} />
  <p><code>rank</code> sorts the records by how likely the answer is yes.</p>
  <Terminal run={sorted} caption={`Recorded from the real command. ${sorted.see}`} />
  <p>Put them in a pipe and the second question reads only what the first kept. <a href="/how-tos/rank-the-inbound-leads/">See the leads how-to</a> for <code>filter</code>, <code>rank</code>, and <code>choose</code> in one pipeline.</p>

  <h2>8. Ask many questions at once</h2>
  <p><code>annotate</code> fills out a form for every record. The form is a question set: any mix of <code>decide</code>, <code>choose</code>, <code>score</code>, and <code>tag</code> questions in one file, under <code>"version": 1</code>.</p>
  <Code code={triageForm.text} status="run" caption={`${triageForm.name}, the question set the run below fills out.`} copy={true} />
  <div class="block">
    <div class="caption"><span class="status run">run</span><span class="grow">{triage.inputFile}, the file the pipeline reads.</span></div>
    <pre>{triage.input}</pre>
  </div>
  {triage.steps.map((step) => <Terminal run={step} caption={`Recorded from the real command. ${HOWTOS.find((h) => h.slug === 'triage-a-support-inbox').see[step.name]}`} />)}
  <p>One message left the pipeline at the <code>filter</code>, so three records reached the form. Each record comes back with one field per question.</p>

  <h2>What you have now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One question you can branch on: exit 0 for yes, 1 for no, 3 for not sure.</li>
    <li>The probability behind it, under <code>--details</code>.</li>
    <li>A bar you set, and a band that sends the middle to a person.</li>
    <li>A question saved to a file, shared by every place that asks it.</li>
    <li>A pipeline that judges a stream and hands each record back with its answers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>ThinkThen answers. Your code acts. It never runs a command and it never writes a file you did not name.</p>

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/reference/">Read the reference</a> · <a href="/functions/">See every function</a> · <a href="/how-tos/">Try a how-to</a> · <a href="/refusals/">See what it will not do</a></p>
</Base>
